# React coding style

Narrows `ts-js-node`'s coding style to React component files. Applies only
to `*.tsx`/`*.jsx` — non-component TypeScript/JavaScript still
comes from `ts-js-node`'s own coding-style rule, not this file.

## Components

- Write function components; a class component is a maintenance
  regression unless the project has no alternative (e.g. an error boundary,
  which still requires a class in current React).
- One component per file for anything exported and reused; a small private
  helper component used only inside its parent file can stay local.
- Name a component file after the component it exports (`UserCard.tsx`
  exports `UserCard`), `PascalCase` for the component, `camelCase` for
  hooks (`useUserCard`) and plain functions.
- Destructure props in the function signature; do not thread a whole
  `props` object through the body when only a few fields are used.
- Type props with an explicit `interface`/`type`, not `React.FC` — `FC`'s
  implicit `children` and weaker generic inference are a known footgun the
  current React types intentionally do not paper over.

## Hooks and state

- Call hooks only at the top level of a component or another hook, never
  inside a condition, loop, or nested function — the react-hooks lint rule
  enforces this; do not add an eslint-disable line to route around it (see
  `rules/security.mdc` and `react-build-fix`'s guardrails).
- Order hooks so the same hooks run in the same order on every render;
  branch inside a hook's own logic (`if (x) return early` after the hook
  call), not around the hook call itself.
- Prefer several focused `useState` calls over one large state object when
  the fields update independently; group into one state (or `useReducer`)
  only when fields change together.
- Name a custom hook's return values by what they mean in the caller
  (`{ data, isLoading, error }`), not by the hook's internal variable
  names.

## Imports and structure

- Group imports: React/framework, third-party, then local — the same
  ordering `ts-js-node`'s coding-style rule already asks for; nothing
  React-specific changes the grouping itself.
- Import hooks and utilities by name (`import { useState } from "react"`);
  do not import the whole `React` namespace unless the project's JSX
  transform requires it (check the project's `tsconfig`/bundler config —
  the classic transform needs `import React from "react"` in scope, the
  automatic transform introduced in React 17 does not).
- Co-locate a component's own styles, tests, and small subcomponents next
  to it under one directory when the project already uses that layout;
  match the existing convention rather than introducing a new one.
